  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington had a rough Monday\u2014down 18% YoY in revenue and down 28% in transaction count. However, the DOW-adjusted analysis shows this is real performance loss, not calendar drift. Capacity was adequate at 5 groomer-days, but core grooming revenue fell 17% week-over-week and add-on attach dropped from 71% to 59%, signaling softer bookings and weaker upsell discipline. The good news: year-to-date you're up 45% in both revenue and appointments versus last year\u2014momentum is real. Hicksville reversed course yesterday, up 40% in revenue on just 2 groomer-days, and MTD performance beats DOW-adjusted expectation by 7.4%. That's operational strength worth noting. However, Hicksville is still down 24% month-to-date versus July, reflecting a structural staffing gap\u2014you're running 1.9 groomer-days per day versus 2.7 last month. That's the root cause, not execution.\n\nThe pattern across both stores: capacity is compressed. Port Washington has drifted from 4.7 to 4.4 groomer-days per month; Hicksville dropped from 2.7 to 1.9. Port Washington's productivity per groomer is holding ($476 MTD versus $511 last month\u2014a 7% slip), while Hicksville is flat ($430 versus $429). Neither store is underperforming relative to bodies on the floor\u2014the issue is fewer bodies. Retail is soft at both locations and inventory holes are growing (X-Small Treat Table zeroed out at Port, 142 units short at Hicksville). Your lapse pools total $388K in annual value; reactivation focus could offset some capacity constraints near-term.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today you have 10 groomer-days across both stores (7 Port, 3 Hicksville) with zero bather hours; tomorrow 10 groomer-days with 7 bather hours at Port; next 7 days 56 groomer-days total (38 Port, 18 Hicksville) with 43 bather hours, all at Port."
